  "plan": {
    "goal": "Culture human proximal tubular epithelial cells in a microchannel reproducing the apical fluid shear stress of the human kidney proximal tubule (~0.1 Pa).",
    "rationale": "Human proximal tubular epithelial cells (PTECs) experience apical fluid shear stress in the range ~0.5-2 dyn/cm² (0.05-0.2 Pa) in vivo. I target the mid-range value of 0.1 Pa (1 dyn/cm²). Using a 400 µm wide x 100 µm high x 20 mm long PDMS channel (culture area ~0.08 cm²), the flow rate required to reach 0.1 Pa wall shear stress is 4.0 µL/min (computed via flow_rate_for_shear_stress with medium viscosity 1e-3 Pa·s). This gives Re = 0.27 (laminar), residence time ~12 s, and pressure drop ~40 Pa — all well within syringe-pump capability. PTECs are seeded at ~1e5 cells/cm² (~8,000 cells/channel) to form a confluent monolayer. NOTE: the proximal tubule FSS target is a literature value (not in the physiology registry) and must be confirmed against the specific PTEC source; the registry has no kidney profile.",
